You are analyzing mathematical statements extracted from a single paper.
You will be given a bibliography (list of cite keys) and a list of statements.
Your task is to find non-obvious inter-paper citations — ones that a simple \cite scanner
would miss. Every citation you report MUST be anchored to a \cite command in the text;
implicit natural-language-only references without a \cite key do not qualify.

**What to look for — the \cite must occur together with:**

A named target (dep_name) — the main goal:
  The citation names a specific result from the cited paper, e.g.:
  "by Theorem 3.2 of \cite{foo}", "\cite[Lemma A.1]{bar}", "the bound in \cite{baz} (Corollary 2)"
  Report the dep_name (e.g. "Theorem 3.2", "Lemma A.1") whenever you can read it from context.
  This is the primary signal — always try to extract dep_name even if the \cite is simple.

In pre_context or post_context — \cite commands with explicit anchors only:
  The \cite must appear with a location-based word ("above", "below", "following",
  "preceding", etc.) or logic-based word ("it follows", "as a consequence", etc.).
  A lone \cite in surrounding text without such an anchor does not qualify.

Each statement has an "id" field and may have the following text fields:
- body: the statement itself
- note: optional bracketed annotation (e.g. theorem name/qualifier)
- proof: the proof of the statement
- pre_context: text immediately before the statement in the source
- post_context: text immediately after the statement in the source

For each citation found, output all five of the following fields — all are required:
- "src": the id of the statement containing the citation
- "cite_key": the exact key from the provided bibliography (never invent a key)
- "dep_name": the specific result being cited, if identifiable
  (e.g. "Theorem 3.2", "Lemma A.1"; null if only the paper in general is cited)
- "location": which field of src contains the citation — one of:
  body, note, proof, pre_context, post_context
- "phrase": verbatim evidence from that field.
  • For body, note, or proof: shortest verbatim excerpt, typically 3–8 words
    (e.g. "by Theorem 3.2 of \cite{foo}", "\cite[Lemma A.1]{bar}").
  • For pre_context or post_context: a full clause of 6–15 words verbatim — never
    a bare \cite alone. Must include the anchor words.
    BAD:  "\cite{foo}"
    GOOD: "The following result strengthens \cite[Theorem 1]{foo} by removing"
  Never paraphrase — all text in "phrase" must appear verbatim in the source field.

Only cite keys present in the provided bibliography are valid.

Respond with valid YAML only, in this exact format:
citations:
- src: "<id>"
  cite_key: "<key>"
  dep_name: "<name or null>"
  location: "<location>"
  phrase: "<phrase>"

If no citations are found, respond with:
citations: []
